All the print methods follow this pattern

https://github.com/tidymodels/parsnip/blob/af46f571c4e7ea07d7a707bc1a12e3567ff93e5e/R/boost_tree.R#L86-L95

What about we create a wrapper so we can do

```r
print_helper <- function(name, x, ...) {
  cat(name, " Specification (", x$mode, ")\n\n", sep = "")
  model_printer(x, ...)
  
  if (!is.null(x$method$fit$args)) {
    cat("Model fit template:\n")
    print(show_call(x))
  }
  invisible(x)
}

print.decision_tree <- function(x, ...) {
  print_helper("Decision Tree Model", x, ...)
}
```

[The package](https://github.com/mlverse/cuda.ml) has cuda-methods for linear regression, SVMs, random forest, and boosting. This would allow the models to be called from parsnip and would enable their tuning etc. 

Assuming that we don't need to write wrappers (for example, there are different functions for regression and classification KNN models), the engine registration code can sit inside of parsnip. 

@yitao-li @skeydan

Some functions require wrappers since the specification has main arguments that map to the underlying function's control object. `rpart` and `C5.0` are good examples. 

For these cases, it is possible to have an engine-specific version of `make_call` that would appropriately insert the main parameters in the appropriate place. This would remove the need for wrappers for these types of models and would reduce some package dependencies. 

(`caret` does this without constructing a call but instead makes a list with all argument values and then uses `do.call` (which is not preferred)).

Using `rpart` as an example, some pseudo code is:

```r
decision_tree.fit_call <- function(spec, args) {
  fun <- spec$method$fit$func["fun"]
  ns  <- spec$method$fit$func["pkg"]
  engine <- spec$engine
  if(engine == "rpart") {
    res <- make_rpart_call(spec, arg)
  } else {
    res <- fit_call.default(spec, arg)
  }
  res  
}

make_rpart_call <- function(fun, ns, args, ...) {
  
  # remove any null or placeholders (`missing_args`) that remain
  discard <-
    vapply(args, function(x)
      is_missing_arg(x) | is.null(x), logical(1))
  args <- args[!discard]
  
  ctrl_args <- args[  names(args) %in% c("cp", "maxdepth") ]
  args      <- args[!(names(args) %in% c("cp", "maxdepth"))]
  
  if (length(ctrl_args) > 0) {
    if (any(names(args) == "control")) {
      # Insert our arguments into the existing `control` object
    } else {
      # Create a clean `control` object and add our arguments. 
      # Attach the new `control` object to `args`.
    }
  }

  if (!is.null(ns) & !is.na(ns)) {
    out <- call2(fun, !!!args, .ns = ns)
  } else
    out <- call2(fun, !!!args)
  out
}
```

Below is `find_varying()`. There is a point where it calls `map_lgl` over a potential list, but if this list is empty, it will return `logical(0)`. This _just happens_ to work because we then call `any(logical(0))` which returns `FALSE` (which is what we want), but I'd rather it actually return the right thing. I think we can just say the following in the marked spot below

```r
if (length(x) > 0) {
   map_lgl(x, find_varying)
}
FALSE
```

```r
function (x) 
{
    if (is_quosure(x)) 
        x <- quo_get_expr(x)
    if (is_varying(x)) {
        return(TRUE)
    }
    else if (is.atomic(x) | is.name(x)) {
        FALSE
    }
    else if (is.call(x) || is.pairlist(x)) {
        for (i in seq_along(x)) {
            if (is_varying(x[[i]])) 
                return(TRUE)
        }
        FALSE
    }
    else if (is.vector(x) | is.list(x)) {
        map_lgl(x, find_varying) # <- RIGHT HERE
    }
    else {
        stop("Don't know how to handle type ", typeof(x), call. = FALSE)
    }
}
```

if you want to see it happening, you can use this code

```r
library(parsnip)
library(recipes)

x <- recipe(Species ~ ., data = iris) %>%
